{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-42771",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Since both fractions have a common denominator of $6$, we can combine them to form one fraction, which gives\n \n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{5}{6}=\\frac{-1+5}{6}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the numerator, we can use either Method 1 or Method 2, shown below.\n\n**Method 1 - Picturing a Number Line**\n\nTo add $5$ to $-1,$ we start at $-1$ on a number line and move $5$ spaces to the right.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14149949/assets/632e4bcce5b619f9a5c4dfb2c414a33da0e9567b7b4d8b15adb439da1b3dd88d.png)\n\nSo, $-1+5=4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{-1+5}{6}=\\frac{4}{6}=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\n**Method 2 - Subtraction in Reverse**\n\nWe can swap the order of the numbers in the numerator, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-1+5}{6}=\\frac{5-1}{6}=\\frac{4}{6}=\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that $-\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{5}{6}=\\frac{2}{3}.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
